The compiler reads the whole program, and every library the program imports, before it
writes a single line of C. Because it sees the whole program, it knows which procedures are
never called, and it leaves them out. It knows which variables are never assigned, and it
treats them as constants. It knows when a loop only ever adds flonums, and it keeps the
flonums in registers.

A small program gets a small executable. A program that never reads a datum does not carry
the reader; a program that never prints a float does not carry the float printer. The
runtime is a set of parts, and the program only links the parts it uses.

Don't take the compiler's word for it: `shikra size` shows what is in the executable, part
by part, so you can see where every byte came from.
